## Personal Setup Rules (Morgan's Pack)

These extend BestPractice's own conventions above; they are this repo's
personal layer (`process/personal/`), not upstream BestPractice, and they
never get exported to the public BestPractice repo.

- **On conflict with BestPractice, this pack wins.** BestPractice sets the
  default; where a rule here and a rule of BestPractice's own genuinely
  disagree on the same point, this pack's rule governs. Where this pack is
  silent, BestPractice's own rule stands undisturbed — this pack only
  narrows or overrides where it actually speaks
  ([`bestpractice-wins`](process/personal/README.md#bestpractice-wins)).

- **Don't duplicate BestPractice.** This pack exists to add to BestPractice
  or override it, not restate it. A bullet here that only repeats what
  BestPractice already establishes on its own gets dropped the next time
  this file is touched, rather than left as a second copy that can drift out
  of sync with the first
  ([`no-duplication`](process/personal/README.md#no-duplication)).

- **The identity facts below are about Morgan specifically — they apply only
  when Morgan is the one actually driving the change.** The `git config
  user.name`/`user.email` identity (below), Buenos Aires as the timezone for
  dates and commit timestamps (below), "Morgan F" as the `NAME` in a file's
  last-updated header (below), the `themorgan` GitHub attribution, Morgan's
  own he/him pronouns, and the `go`/`merge` shorthand (further below) are
  facts about one person, not defaults for whoever happens to be committing.
  When a different GitHub user or person drives a change in a repo this pack
  is installed into — co-authoring, running their own session, opening their
  own PR — none of this carries over automatically: don't attribute their
  commits to "Morgan F", don't assume their timezone is Buenos Aires, don't
  refer to them as "he" absent them saying so, and don't require the exact
  "go"/"merge" wording from them. Fall back to BestPractice's own generic
  default instead (ask, don't assume) for anyone who isn't Morgan
  ([`morgan-scope`](process/personal/README.md#morgan-scope)). Each bullet
  below that states one of these facts carries a short pointer back to this
  one.

- **Commits are always authored "Morgan F".** Use Morgan's own email
  address: `git config user.name "Morgan F"` and `git config user.email
  "morgan@westegg.com"` once per clone
  ([tools/bootstrap.sh](tools/bootstrap.sh) does this automatically). This
  replaces, rather than defers to, BestPractice's own "ask before the first
  commit" default — the identity is already decided, so don't ask. It
  replaces only the *identity* half: this pack says nothing about
  co-authorship, so BestPractice's own `Co-Authored-By:` trailer naming the
  assistant still applies
  ([`bestpractice-wins`](process/personal/README.md#bestpractice-wins) —
  where this pack is silent, BestPractice stands). This whole bullet is one
  of the Morgan-specific facts scoped above — for anyone who isn't Morgan,
  ask instead ([`commit-author`](process/personal/README.md#commit-author)).

- **Every date is a Buenos Aires local calendar date**, not the session's
  system clock. Two places this bites:
  - Any date written into a document ("as of / verified `<date>`" per
    BestPractice practice 16, a file's last-updated header below) uses
    `America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires` local time.
  - Git commit timestamps: prefix the commit command with
    `TZ="America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires"` so the recorded author/committer
    offset is Buenos Aires', not the container's —
    `TZ="America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires" git commit -m "..."`.
  (Argentina has held UTC−3 year-round, no daylight saving, since 2009 —
  verified 2026-08-21; re-verify if this rule is ever touched and the fact
  seems dated.) Buenos Aires is *Morgan's* timezone specifically — one of
  the facts scoped above — not a default for someone else driving the change
  ([`buenos-aires-dates`](process/personal/README.md#buenos-aires-dates)).

- **Files carry a "last updated" timestamp, who made it, and a version
  number — near the top, when reasonable.** For a Markdown file: `<!-- Last
  updated: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS (Buenos Aires) by NAME, to version N -->` as
  the first line — a full timestamp, hour/minute/second, not just a calendar
  date; a bare date can't tell two same-day edits apart. `NAME` is whoever
  made the edit — "Morgan F" when Morgan is the one driving the change (one
  of the facts scoped above), the same name every commit is already authored
  as, so the header shows who last touched the file without a `git blame`;
  otherwise `NAME` is simply whoever actually made the edit. `N` is a plain
  integer counter private to that one file: 1 the first time the header is
  added, +1 each subsequent time the file's content changes. The "to version
  N" phrasing is deliberate — it names that file's own version, not a
  repo-wide release number, so bumping it is a one-file edit. That's also
  why installing this rule doesn't mean adding the header to every existing
  file at once: a file only picks up (or bumps) its header when it's already
  being edited for some other reason — **only the files actually changed**,
  never a repo-wide sweep. For a script or config format with comments, the
  equivalent in that syntax. Skip it where a leading comment would break the
  file (JSON has none) or where the file is a vendored, byte-for-byte copy
  under `process/upstream/` — that tree must track upstream exactly, never a
  local edit. Update the line whenever you touch the file's content, not its
  formatting alone — same trigger for bumping `N`
  ([`file-header`](process/personal/README.md#file-header)).

- **An auto-generated file is named and marked so nobody edits it by
  hand.** A file a script or process produces — regenerated on demand,
  never meant to carry a hand edit — gets two markers together, not
  either alone: its own filename carries `.generated.` immediately
  before its extension (`SUMMARY.generated.md`, generalized as
  `.generated.<ext>`), visible in a listing or a diff without opening
  the file; and its first line (or comment-syntax equivalent) reads
  `<!-- AUTO-GENERATED by <script/process> from <source file(s)> — DO
  NOT EDIT BY HAND. Regenerate with: <command> -->`, replacing
  [`file-header`](process/personal/README.md#file-header)'s own header
  on that file rather than sitting beside it. Neither is retroactive —
  a file only picks this up once a real generation process for it
  exists
  ([`generated-file-marker`](process/personal/README.md#generated-file-marker)).

- **Commit messages link the assistant session where the change was
  planned.** Add a trailer, `Session: <url>` — for Claude Code,
  `https://claude.ai/code/session_<ID>` (the running session's own ID; ask
  the harness for it if it isn't already visible). For an unattended run
  with no chat session behind it (the sync workflows below), use that run's
  own URL instead. If a tool genuinely has no shareable link, say so in the
  trailer (`Session: none available (<tool>)`) rather than dropping it
  silently — the point is a reviewer can always tell whether a link was
  considered and skipped, or just forgotten
  ([`session-trailer`](process/personal/README.md#session-trailer)).

- **Decide small calls yourself; only stop for big ones.** Default to
  continuing, not asking. When a judgment call is needed to keep moving and
  it's small or moderate — a default value, a phrasing choice, picking
  between two reasonable implementations, an ambiguity that doesn't change
  the shape of what gets delivered — make the call and note it in **both**
  the normal end-of-work reply that already lists files touched, *and* the
  commit message itself, under a "Judgment calls made:" heading (the same
  pattern the sync workflows already use for their own "Judgment calls to
  review:" heading — see the sync bullets below). The chat reply is easy to
  lose once a thread scrolls on; the commit message is what survives into
  `git log` and the PR diff, so it stays visible to someone reviewing the
  merged PR later, not just to someone reading the reply in the moment. Skip
  the heading on a commit that made no judgment calls — don't pad every
  commit with "none" — but never leave a real one out of the commit message.
  Reserve stopping and asking for calls that are genuinely big: hard or
  costly to undo, change what gets delivered or to whom, spend real money,
  touch credentials or production, or are a toss-up two reasonable people
  would clearly land differently on
  ([`small-calls`](process/personal/README.md#small-calls)).

- **Push-back mode: argue, don't just comply, on writing-and-thinking work —
  never on technical or code work.** On work whose deliverable is prose
  meant to persuade or be judged by a human reader as an argument (a memo, a
  strategy call, a brainstorm entry, an essay, a decision writeup): argue a
  genuine counter-case before building on a stance as stated, and flag a
  serious unresolved disagreement before calling a piece done. Never applies
  to code, scripts, configuration, infrastructure, or debugging — including
  a technical sub-conversation inside a session that also does
  writing-and-thinking work elsewhere. This is not a quota: never push back
  just to push back, and hand over a piece with no real problem as-is rather
  than manufacturing an objection. Reserve it for where it could actually
  help in a serious or deep way. Distinct from a judgment call ("decide
  small calls yourself" above): a judgment call is a small ambiguity to
  resolve; this is a real, contested disagreement worth surfacing even when
  nothing is blocking. Unlike the rest of this pack, this one rule isn't
  mechanically checkable — it relies on honest judgment about whether a
  disagreement is real, so its calibration is something to notice over time,
  not something a script can verify
  ([`push-back`](process/personal/README.md#push-back)).

- **Trim iteratively-edited prose on two triggers, not a running count.**
  Prose that's revised repeatedly across a conversation tends to only grow —
  each pass adds a clause without removing what it made redundant. Rather
  than track edit counts or length baselines (state disproportionate to the
  problem), trim on two cheap, state-free triggers: immediately after any
  substantial edit to a paragraph (a rewritten sentence, or roughly a
  sentence or more added), give just that paragraph a quick trim; and
  independent of edit size, if a paragraph has grown noticeably longer than
  the point it's making warrants, trim it before calling the piece done —
  this catches slow drift from edits too small individually to trigger the
  first. Scoped the same as push-back mode above: prose meant to be read and
  judged by a human, not code, scripts, configuration, or other work meant
  to run. Not scoped to Morgan — a writing-quality habit, like
  [`branch-links`](process/personal/README.md#branch-links),
  [`rule-links`](process/personal/README.md#rule-links), and
  [`header-caps`](process/personal/README.md#header-caps)
  ([`trim-prose`](process/personal/README.md#trim-prose)).

- **Cocreated list items stay roughly comparable in length — default
  short.** When cowriting a list, or a document with several short-ish
  sections in a row that function like one, keep each item to
  approximately the same length as its neighbors. This matters most during
  revision: it's natural to edit one point, expand it while you're in
  there, and leave the others untouched, even though nothing about that
  item's actual importance changed. If one item genuinely carries more
  weight, check whether it makes more sense to pull it out into its own
  section rather than let it balloon in place. Watch for this both when a
  list is first drafted and every time an existing item in one gets
  revised. Absent a specific instruction otherwise, default to the shorter
  side for list items and list-like sections, not the longer side. A
  strong preference, not a hard rule — a list can be genuinely uneven in
  what its items need to say. Scoped the same as
  [`push-back`](process/personal/README.md#push-back) and
  [`trim-prose`](process/personal/README.md#trim-prose): cowritten prose
  and documents, not code, scripts, or configuration
  ([`list-item-parity`](process/personal/README.md#list-item-parity)).

- **`go` or `merge` as a standalone final sentence means: commit and merge
  to main.** If Morgan's message, at a point where you've said you're ready,
  ends with `go` or `merge` as its own sentence — on its own line, or set
  off by a preceding sentence-ending punctuation mark, case-insensitive —
  that's authorization, right there, to commit the pending work and merge it
  into the default branch, using this repo's usual conventions (identity,
  timestamp, session trailer, gates), without asking again first. "That was
  perfect. merge" and "Go." both count; "let's merge the two lists" and "go
  check the logs" don't — the word has to stand alone as the message's last
  sentence, not appear as part of a longer one. If it's ambiguous whether
  the trailing word is this shorthand or ordinary language in context, ask
  rather than assume. This is shorthand for the same authorization the
  "merge only when the user says so" default above already asks for
  ([`go-merge`](process/personal/README.md#go-merge)). This exact wording is
  Morgan's own shorthand — one of the facts scoped above — so don't expect
  or require it from anyone else; fall back to asking.

- **TODO.md gate — step 0c of the merge runbook, before every push.**
  Immediately after the export gate (0b): re-read this thread's discussion
  against [TODO.md](TODO.md). Add any idea that came up but never got a
  line; remove or check off anything this branch has now implemented.
  `TODO.md` drifting out of sync with what was actually decided is the
  specific failure this gate exists to catch — do it every push, not just
  when something feels stale
  ([`todo-gate`](process/personal/README.md#todo-gate)).

- **A light check runs on every commit path**
  ([.github/workflows/light-check.yml](.github/workflows/light-check.yml),
  [process/personal/tools/light_check.py](process/personal/tools/light_check.py))
  — conflict markers, invalid JSON/YAML/Python syntax, secret-shaped
  strings, broken doc links, and (repo-wide, every run) that
  `process/personal/` is actually vendored: `process/manifest_personal.json`
  exists, parses, has entries, and every entry's file exists on disk —
  catches the pack having been copied in rather than installed per
  [`install`](process/personal/README.md#install). Run it yourself before
  committing, the same way you already run
  [doc_lint.py](process/upstream/tools/doc_lint.py) and
  [practice_audit.py](process/upstream/tools/practice_audit.py); CI runs it
  too, so it binds even if a session forgets
  ([`light-check`](process/personal/README.md#light-check)).

- **The deep check — every audit, plus an open-ended coherence review**
  ([`deep-check`](process/personal/README.md#deep-check)). The merge
  runbook's step 3 already runs
  [doc_lint.py](process/upstream/tools/doc_lint.py),
  [light_check.py](process/personal/tools/light_check.py), and
  [practice_audit.py](process/upstream/tools/practice_audit.py) before every
  merge — that is the deep check's mechanical half, and it is not optional.
  Its other half is a read of the **whole repo** against itself — this
  repo's own documents, the vendored `process/personal/` tree, and the fit
  between them; never `process/upstream/`, which is a mirror, not ours to
  edit. Look for contradictions, stale slugs / practice numbers / file
  references / click-paths, fragments left behind by an earlier edit, the
  same rule restated in full in several places, detail out of proportion to
  the point it makes, formatting and spacing drift (heading levels and
  capitalization, a bullet missing its blank line, ragged tables, stray
  blank lines or trailing whitespace, a stale "last updated" header), a rule
  this pack asks of every repo it installs into that this repo doesn't yet
  follow itself, and [TODO.md](TODO.md) entries already done or no longer
  relevant. **That list is a starting point, not a specification: report
  anything that makes the repo harder to trust or follow, including problems
  no bullet names — and add a bullet for the ones that will recur.** Run
  both halves when Morgan asks for a "deep check" by name, and after work
  that invites drift — rules added or reordered, a rule that changed shape,
  an install into a new repo, a merge that resolved conflicts across several
  shared files. Fix what it turns up in the same pass, re-run the audits
  afterward (the fixes themselves can break a link), and give anything
  deliberately left alone a line in [TODO.md](TODO.md). Deliberately not a
  per-commit gate — the light check above is what carries that cadence.

- **Don't repeat the same backlog explanation every run.** The merge
  runbook's step 3 above can turn up a real pre-existing backlog (a bare
  filename, an unglossed acronym) that predates the current edit and isn't
  gated. Explaining it away with the same sentence — "pre-existing warnings
  only, unrelated to my edit" — before every commit is repeating a
  disclaimer that never changes and never informs the next step, since the
  runbook already moves straight to checking branch state and committing
  regardless. This isn't a rule to stay silent about checks in general — a
  plain "checks passed" is fine, and often exactly what's wanted, in an
  end-of-work summary or wherever reporting status is the natural thing to
  do. What to drop is the recurring aside walking through *why* the same
  known backlog doesn't matter; a run that actually fails, or a warning the
  current edit newly introduces, is always worth flagging. Not scoped to
  Morgan — a communication habit, like
  [`branch-links`](process/personal/README.md#branch-links)
  ([`quiet-checks`](process/personal/README.md#quiet-checks)).

- **Agent-relevant instructions in a README or other key file also go in
  `AGENTS.md`.** A README, `CONTRIBUTING.md`,
  [GETTING_STARTED.md](GETTING_STARTED.md), or any other key file can pick
  up its own operational instructions over time — a setup step, a gotcha, a
  rule about how to work in the repo — written where a human reader would
  look, not where an agent is told to look. When such a file gains an
  instruction useful for an agent to know (the operational kind: how to
  build it, a constraint on how to work, a step an agent would otherwise
  miss — not general project description or end-user documentation), fold
  the same instruction into `AGENTS.md` too, in its own words if that reads
  better there. This runs both directions: whether the instruction lands
  first in the other file and needs pulling into `AGENTS.md`, or is written
  into `AGENTS.md` directly and never mirrored back to where a human would
  expect to read it, either way the same instruction ends up in both places.
  It doesn't run the other way for content that only belongs in one place —
  a README's marketing framing has no business in `AGENTS.md`, and
  `AGENTS.md`'s own meta-structure has no business padding out a README.
  Check for this at the capture gate (merge runbook step 0) the same way any
  other captured decision gets folded in
  ([`mirror-into-agents`](process/personal/README.md#mirror-into-agents)).

- **A new pack rule lands in reading-order position, never appended.** When
  adding a rule to the personal pack: pick where it belongs by subject among
  the existing groups, not the end of the file; assign it a permanent slug
  and anchor; renumber every rule after the insertion point in both the
  headings and the group table, checking for any stale total-count mention
  along the way ([`no-stale-counts`](process/personal/README.md#no-stale-counts));
  mirror the new rule into `AGENTS.md`, this template, `MAP.md`, and
  `NEW_REPO_SETUP.md`, in the same reading-order position; then re-run the
  light check and record the addition in `TODO.md`. Not mechanically
  enforced past the slug/anchor check — the rest rides on
  [`deep-check`](process/personal/README.md#deep-check)'s review. Not scoped
  to Morgan — a documentation-quality habit, like
  [`rule-links`](process/personal/README.md#rule-links)
  ([`new-rule-placement`](process/personal/README.md#new-rule-placement)).

- **A mentioned branch is always a link.** BestPractice's own practice 11
  (doc references are links) covers files at the repo's current tree; it
  doesn't reach a git branch, since a branch is a ref, not a path. Whenever
  a reply names a git branch — not only in the Files-touched footer practice
  12 already requires, but anywhere in running text, a status update, a
  decision note — link it, the same reasoning as practice 11 extended to
  refs. Link to that branch's tree view on whichever host the repo lives on
  (for GitHub: `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/tree/<branch-name>`); a
  branch in some other repo a reply happens to mention gets linked on that
  repo's own host, not this one's. A bare branch name in backticks or plain
  prose is the failure mode this rule exists to catch. Not scoped to Morgan
  the way the identity facts above are — a documentation habit, not a fact
  about one person, so it applies regardless of who is driving the session
  ([`branch-links`](process/personal/README.md#branch-links)).

- **A mentioned rule, item, or destination is always a link.** Extends
  practice 11 and
  [`branch-links`](process/personal/README.md#branch-links) into the
  general principle both are instances of: anything mentioned that has a
  destination gets a link to it, the first time it's mentioned — a repo
  file (practice 11's own rule), a rule of this pack, a BestPractice
  practice number, a [TODO.md](TODO.md) entry, a
  [GLOSSARY.md](GLOSSARY.md) term, a decision record, a
  `process/manifest*.json` entry, a git branch
  ([`branch-links`](process/personal/README.md#branch-links)), a commit
  (its commit page, short SHA as the link text), a PR or issue (its page —
  GitHub auto-links `#43` in comments but not in repo markdown), or an
  external service, tool, spec, or doc page named in prose. **This covers
  replies in chat as much as files in the repo** — a reply naming a rule or
  a PR without linking it is the same failure, and a reply is the easier
  place to forget. Link on *first* use in a given document or reply, then
  use the plain name; never inside a code span or block, where a path or
  URL is a value, not a reference. **The pack's own rules have one citation
  form, and it isn't optional:** ``
  [`deep-check`](process/personal/README.md#deep-check) `` from elsewhere
  in the repo, `` [`deep-check`](#deep-check) `` inside
  [process/personal/README.md](process/personal/README.md) itself, and ``
  `deep-check` (process/personal/README.md#deep-check) `` where markdown
  links don't render (a script comment, a YAML prompt, a manifest note).
  The slug is the rule's permanent name; the number on its heading is
  positional and moves whenever the file is reorganized, so citing a rule
  by number is an error
  [light_check.py](process/personal/tools/light_check.py) fails on — as is
  a slug that resolves to no anchor. `§` still belongs to a document we
  don't control the numbering of — BestPractice's own sections
  (`INSTALL.md §2`), or another vendored pack's own numbered file
  (SoundHuman's `HUMAN_VOICE_RULES.md §17`) — cited with that file's name
  right beside it. light_check.py recognizes the shape generically, not a
  fixed list of filenames, so a newly vendored pack needs no separate
  update, and the same file-qualified form is the general citation for any
  other document's own reference-able, independently-numbered rules or
  protocols too — a bare `README.md` doesn't count, since that always means
  this pack's own rules. The rest of the kinds above can't be checked
  mechanically, so they ride on
  [`deep-check`](process/personal/README.md#deep-check)'s review instead.
  Not scoped to Morgan — a documentation habit, like
  [`branch-links`](process/personal/README.md#branch-links)
  ([`rule-links`](process/personal/README.md#rule-links)).

- **In chat and PR/commit text, every file mention is a clickable link —
  even inside code.** [`rule-links`](process/personal/README.md#rule-links)
  links a file the first time it's mentioned, using a relative link, and
  leaves a filename inside a code span alone. Both are right for a document
  actually committed to the repo; neither holds for a chat reply or text
  aimed straight at GitHub (a PR description, a commit message) — those
  aren't part of the repo tree, so a relative link doesn't resolve, and a
  reader skimming a long reply or PR body wants whichever mention is in
  front of them to work, not the first one. For those two surfaces only:
  every mention of a specific file is a clickable, absolute GitHub URL
  (`https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/blob/<branch>/<path>`) — staying
  inside its code span (`` `AGENTS.md` `` becomes ``
  [`AGENTS.md`](https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/blob/<branch>/AGENTS.md)
  ``) rather than growing a separate marker like a trailing 🔗. Branch: an
  open PR's own head branch while it's open, the default branch once merged
  or when the reply isn't tied to a particular PR — the same choice the
  "Files touched" footer already makes for its own two links. Not
  mechanically enforced — a chat reply and a PR/commit body both live
  outside what any check here can scan, so this rides on session habit alone
  ([`file-mention-links`](process/personal/README.md#file-mention-links)).

- **Don't state a count that will drift — describe it instead.** A sentence
  citing an exact count of something that changes over time (how many
  numbered rules this pack has, how many warnings a lint tool currently
  reports) reads precisely today and goes stale the moment that thing
  changes, with nothing to flag it — found via
  [NEW_REPO_SETUP.md](NEW_REPO_SETUP.md) saying "Twenty-nine numbered
  sections" while the pack already had thirty. When the exact count isn't
  the point being made, drop it rather than restate it: "numbered sections,"
  not "twenty-nine numbered sections." Doesn't apply to a version number, a
  date, or a count genuinely maintained alongside the thing it counts (the
  personal pack's own rule table). Not mechanically enforced — rides on
  [`deep-check`](process/personal/README.md#deep-check)'s review, like
  [`rule-links`](process/personal/README.md#rule-links)'s non-slug citation
  kinds
  ([`no-stale-counts`](process/personal/README.md#no-stale-counts)).

- **Header capitalization: pick one consistent schema, default to NY Times
  headline style.** BestPractice's own Conventions section above already
  requires headers and subheaders at the same rank to follow one
  capitalization style, never mixed — but leaves *which* style to the repo.
  Absent a documented reason to pick something else, use **NY Times
  headline-style capitalization**: capitalize principal words (nouns, verbs,
  adjectives, adverbs, pronouns), lowercase minor words (articles, short
  prepositions, coordinating conjunctions) except at the very start or end
  of the header, consistently across every header and subheader in the
  document — the same default RepoPersonalPreferences follows for its own
  headers. A repo may still choose a different schema — document the choice
  inline wherever this repo's own header-consistency rule is stated, the
  same way, so a later session has something to check against
  ([`header-caps`](process/personal/README.md#header-caps)).

- **Scrub sensitive characterizations of real people, unless told
  otherwise.** Assume whoever is driving a session here talks about real
  people — friends, colleagues, prospective partners — as candidly and
  directly as with a trusted collaborator, and that candor is not, on its
  own, safe to carry into anything committed: found the hard way when a
  direct, off-the-cuff description of a real acquaintance made it into a
  committed document in a dependent repo, was shown to that person, and read
  back more bluntly than intended. Before committing a document, brainstorm
  entry, code comment, or commit message, assume the specific real people it
  describes may eventually read it themselves. Where a description is
  unusually direct, strong, negative, or otherwise sensitive enough that the
  person might wince reading it, don't commit it as given — soften it (a
  euphemism that keeps the substance, not the edge — "very well-connected"
  becomes something like "given the team's strong network") or ask first
  when no softened phrasing preserves what the point needs. Doesn't reach
  the conversation itself, only what gets written down, and doesn't apply to
  ordinary, neutral, complimentary, or already-public description. Not
  mechanically enforced, like [`push-back`](process/personal/README.md#push-back)
  — rides on the session's own judgment and
  [`deep-check`](process/personal/README.md#deep-check)'s later review
  ([`sensitive-characterization-scrub`](process/personal/README.md#sensitive-characterization-scrub)).

- **Private repo names and specifics get scrubbed before anything vendors or
  is shared.** Whoever maintains the personal pack's own source repo
  (RepoPersonalPreferences) may describe the situation that prompted a rule
  only in general terms ("a dependent repo," "an earlier project") inside
  anything that actually ships elsewhere — everything under
  `process/personal/` and `NEW_REPO_SETUP.md` — never by a private repo's
  real name, URL, or identifying internal details. The full, specific
  account belongs only in a record that never leaves that source repo (its
  own TODO.md decision records, say). Not something to act on in a dependent
  repo's own work, but
  [process/personal/tools/light_check.py](process/personal/tools/light_check.py)
  (vendored here too) still runs the same check, against a blocklist of
  known private repo names, as a backstop if one is ever pasted in by
  mistake
  ([`private-repo-scrub`](process/personal/README.md#private-repo-scrub)).

- **LLM integrations stay platform-neutral; OpenRouter is the default
  assumption.** Any system this pack helps set up that talks to an LLM
  should be built against a provider-neutral interface — model name, token,
  and base URL as swappable config, not hard-wired to one vendor's SDK or
  auth shape — so switching providers, or using whichever token is on hand,
  is a config change, not a rewrite. Absent other instruction, assume the
  credential in hand is an OpenRouter token, not a given vendor's own key;
  OpenRouter's API is itself OpenAI-request-shaped *(verified 2026-08-22)*,
  so it's also a sensible default shape for the neutral interface itself
  ([`llm-neutral`](process/personal/README.md#llm-neutral)).

- **Always fail gracefully.** Any code you write or set up here anticipates
  its own common failure modes rather than letting them surface as an
  unhandled crash. Before shipping code that depends on something outside
  its own control — a required config or environment variable, a file that's
  supposed to exist, a network call, a third-party credential — check for
  the absence or failure case on purpose and degrade in a way the caller can
  act on: a clear error message naming exactly what's missing and how to fix
  it, a documented fallback/default, or a clean non-zero exit — never a raw
  stack trace, a silent wrong answer, or a hang
  ([`fail-gracefully`](process/personal/README.md#fail-gracefully)).

- **This repo's default branch is `main`, checked once at install — or, for
  a brand-new blank repo, set by how the very first commit is made.** For an
  *existing* repo whose default branch isn't already `main` (GitHub only
  defaults a freshly-created repo to `main` on its own — a repo that
  predates that default, or arrived some other way, can still sit on
  `master` or something else): set it once — via the GitHub API's
  repo-update endpoint where the session's tools reach that far, otherwise
  as a one-click item (Settings → General → Default branch) disclosed in
  [GETTING_STARTED.md](GETTING_STARTED.md)'s administrator section like any
  other click-path. For a *brand-new, blank* repo with no branches yet,
  there's nothing to check or set at all — make this repo's very first
  commit directly on a branch literally named `main` and push that first,
  not a feature or planning branch; GitHub adopts the first branch ever
  pushed to an empty repo as its default automatically. Only after that
  first commit lands on `main` does the usual feature-branch-plus-PR
  workflow start applying
  ([`default-branch`](process/personal/README.md#default-branch)). One-time
  only — no need to recheck it once set.

- **`process/scrub_blocklist.txt` stays blank at install — never ask, never
  remind.** BestPractice's own install procedure asks for private vocabulary
  to seed this file before any check-in to the public BestPractice repo.
  This pack overrides that default: Morgan doesn't use the
  check-in-to-public-BestPractice path in repos this pack installs into, so
  installing BestPractice here never asks him for blocklist content and
  never volunteers a seeded draft on his own behalf — write the file with
  just its header comment and no entries, and don't bring it up again. If
  Morgan ever wants the feature, he'll say so himself
  ([`blank-blocklist`](process/personal/README.md#blank-blocklist)).

- **In a content-oriented repo, group deliverable content under a
  subdirectory once root gets cluttered — a recommendation, not a rule.**
  When root accumulates three or more documents that are the deliverable
  content itself (not the navigation layer — [MAP.md](MAP.md),
  [GLOSSARY.md](GLOSSARY.md), [TODO.md](TODO.md), a README, an `AGENTS.md`,
  [GETTING_STARTED.md](GETTING_STARTED.md), and `VOICE.md`/`STYLEGUIDE.md`
  where installed, which always stay at root), consider grouping that
  content under one or more named subdirectories — say `book/` for a
  manuscript and `brainstorm/` for the notes that back its decisions.
  Doesn't apply to a code-oriented repo, where root clutter is a different
  problem with its own existing conventions. Nothing checks for this — no
  audit, no gate — and it's never retroactive on its own: raise it as a
  judgment call (at the capture gate, or when a new content-oriented repo
  first takes shape) rather than forcing a restructure and its link-churn on
  an existing repo just because the rule now exists
  ([`content-subdirs`](process/personal/README.md#content-subdirs)).

- **A scheduled check keeps BestPractice itself current — weekly by default,
  one line to switch to daily**
  ([.github/workflows/bestpractice-upstream-sync.yml](.github/workflows/bestpractice-upstream-sync.yml),
  from
  [process/personal/templates/github-actions/upstream-sync.yml.template](process/personal/templates/github-actions/upstream-sync.yml.template)):
  unattended, it takes any upstream update, integrates it with its own
  judgment, and merges it — recording every judgment call in the commit
  message. A session that notices the sync's PR merged with judgment calls
  listed should skim them like any other change; they are flagged precisely
  because they're the ones worth a second look. Authenticates with either
  `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (a Pro/Max subscriber's token from `claude
  setup-token`) or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — only one is required
  ([`bestpractice-sync`](process/personal/README.md#bestpractice-sync)).

- **A second scheduled check keeps the personal pack itself current — same
  weekly-default, daily-optional cadence**
  (`.github/workflows/personal-pack-sync.yml`, from
  [process/personal/templates/github-actions/personal-pack-sync.yml.template](process/personal/templates/github-actions/personal-pack-sync.yml.template)):
  same shape as the BestPractice sync above, but for `process/personal/`
  against its own source, RepoPersonalPreferences (private —
  [process/manifest_personal.json](process/manifest_personal.json)'s
  `upstream.repo`/`upstream.commit` track it the same way
  [process/manifest.json](process/manifest.json) tracks BestPractice). It
  needs its own repository secret, `PERSONAL_PACK_TOKEN`, since that source
  repo is private — see [`pack-sync`](process/personal/README.md#pack-sync).
  That secret can't be set by any session; every run of the workflow's
  `check` job warns (a `::warning::` Actions annotation) if the secret is
  missing — a per-run smoke test, not just a one-time install reminder — but
  if it's ever missing, say so plainly in your own reply too rather than
  assuming it's handled.

- **Both scheduled syncs skip cleanly, not loudly, when neither Claude
  credential is set.** Both check whether `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` or
  `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set before attempting the update; if their source
  moved but both are blank, the `update` job is skipped and a `::warning::`
  lands in that run's summary instead of an auth failure. **If asked to run
  one of these syncs manually** (or you notice mid-thread that one is
  overdue and the user wants it done now), you don't need either secret at
  all — you're already Claude — so just take the update yourself,
  in-session, following the same steps the workflow would, then remind the
  user once, plainly, to set `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` or
  `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` so future runs don't need to be asked for by hand.

- **A session-start notice asks about drift immediately, not at the end.**
  `tools/bootstrap.sh` runs both freshness notices on every session start
  now, no model call needed: `checkin.py fresh` (already wired,
  BestPractice's own, practice 13) and `pack_sync.py fresh`
  ([process/personal/tools/pack_sync.py](process/personal/tools/pack_sync.py),
  wired per [`install`](process/personal/README.md#install) step 4, from
  [process/personal/templates/bootstrap-freshness.sh.template](process/personal/templates/bootstrap-freshness.sh.template)).
  Each prints a one-line notice only when its source has moved —
  `process/manifest.json`'s recorded BestPractice commit against
  BestPractice's actual HEAD, or `process/manifest_personal.json`'s recorded
  commit against RepoPersonalPreferences's actual HEAD — silent otherwise,
  never a gate. Because this runs as part of the same session-start hook
  that already sets up the environment, raise whichever notice fired as part
  of catching the member up, not something saved for the end. **A fired
  notice is also persisted, not just printed:** whichever half fires,
  `tools/bootstrap.sh` also runs `python3
  process/personal/tools/pack_sync.py record <source> "<notice>"`, writing
  it into this repo's own [TODO.md](TODO.md) under a `## Pending Drift
  Reviews` heading (created on first use; re-firing the same source updates
  its one open entry in place rather than duplicating it). A stdout-only
  notice can lose a priority fight against whatever task is already in front
  of a session and go unaddressed — that's exactly what happened in a
  dependent repo, 2026-08-27 (the real name stays out of anything vendored —
  see [`private-repo-scrub`](process/personal/README.md#private-repo-scrub))
  — so `light_check.py` now also warns, on every commit, while any `Pending
  Drift Reviews` entry stays open. Resolve one with `python3
  process/personal/tools/pack_sync.py resolve <source> [note]` once its
  drift has actually been reviewed — taken, or deliberately deferred — which
  checks it off rather than deleting it. Taking either update stays
  deliberate whenever it's raised — never without being asked, since that's
  the one deliberate difference from the scheduled syncs above, which merge
  unattended. If asked to proceed, take the update the same way a
  manually-requested sync already does, opened as a PR the user approves and
  merges themselves rather than auto-merged. This doesn't replace either
  scheduled sync — they still bound the gap between sessions when nobody
  opens the repo; this only closes the narrower gap where a session (and a
  person to ask) is already here. **Fallback, for whenever a session-start
  notice didn't fire or drift happened mid-session:** repeat the same cheap
  `git ls-remote` comparisons at the end of the session too, right after the
  merge runbook's own steps. **This also has to survive an indirect
  install:** the comparisons above assume the repo is fully installed, which
  isn't guaranteed when either tree arrived some way other than a session
  running the install procedure — a fork, a template-repo copy, or a repo
  vendored from an *already-dependent* repo rather than from canonical
  source. So this same check also verifies, whenever `process/upstream/`
  and/or `process/personal/` is present: does
  `.github/workflows/bestpractice-upstream-sync.yml` exist, does
  `.github/workflows/personal-pack-sync.yml` exist, does
  `tools/bootstrap.sh` actually carry both freshness snippets, and does this
  repo's own `AGENTS.md` actually carry these woven-in sections? If
  something's missing, that's an incomplete install, not drift — say so
  plainly and offer to finish it now, sourced from the canonical repo (the
  public BestPractice repo; RepoPersonalPreferences for the pack),
  regardless of which repo the files on disk actually arrived from
  ([`drift-notice`](process/personal/README.md#drift-notice)).

- **Unattended automation reports its own blockers as a GitHub issue, not
  only a log line.** Any scheduled/unattended job in this repo that hits
  something blocking it — a missing or revoked secret, an unexpected
  failure, anything stopping it from finishing its normal work — opens (or
  comments on an existing open one, so recurrences don't pile up duplicates)
  a GitHub issue via `python3
  process/personal/tools/report_automation_issue.py <label> "<title>"
  "<body>"`, in addition to the existing `::warning::` Actions annotation.
  An annotation only lives inside one workflow run; an issue persists in the
  repo and rides GitHub's own notification system (email, mobile push) for
  free. Needs `issues: write` on the calling job and a `GH_TOKEN`
  environment variable (`${{ github.token }}` is enough) — no new secret, no
  repository-settings toggle. Fails gracefully if `gh` isn't authenticated
  or the call errors: prints a WARN, exits 0, never turns a failure to
  *report* a blocker into a second, more confusing one. Already wired into
  both scheduled syncs' missing-credential paths
  ([`automation-issues`](process/personal/README.md#automation-issues)).
